</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rocha</surname><given-names>EP</given-names></name><name><surname>Cornet</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Michel</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Comparative and evolutionary analysis of the bacterial homologous recombination systems</article-title><source>PLoS Genet.</source><year>2005</year><volume>1</volume><fpage>e15</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16132081</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B13"><label>13</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Morimatsu</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Kowalczykowski</surname><given-names>SC</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>RecFOR proteins load RecA protein onto gapped DNA to accelerate DNA strand exchange: a universal step of recombinational repair</article-title><source>Mol. Cell</source><year>2003</year><volume>11</volume><fpage>1337</fpage><lpage>1347</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12769856</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B14"><label>14</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bork</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name><name><surname>Cox</surname><given-names>MM</given-names></name><name><surname>Inman</surname><given-names>RB</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The RecOR proteins modulate RecA protein function at 5&#x02032; ends of single-stranded DNA</article-title><source>Embo. J.</source><year>2001</year><volume>20</volume><fpage>7313</fpage><lpage>7322</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11743007</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B15"><label>15</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Webb</surname><given-names>BL</given-names></name><name><surname>Cox</sur